From bc3088a897a3c7484ac386bb2842fed13a8694bc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 6 Sep 2026 14:38:19 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 4/6] feat(telemetry): an ingest path a real machine can be
 built against
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

POST /api/telemetry took any JSON from anyone on the internet and inserted it.
Now it authenticates the device, stamps provenance from the credential, absorbs
retries, dead-letters what it refuses, and tells the machine what it holds.

  lib/telemetry/ingest.ts   the contract, as a pure function of an injected
                            store. Who may write, what a duplicate is, what
                            happens to a poison event — none of it touches the
                            network, so all of it is testable.
  lib/db/service.ts         server-only service-role client. Throws if
                            evaluated in a browser; refuses to start rather
                            than falling back to a placeholder project, which
                            is the pattern in lib/db/client.ts:19-20 that let a
                            completely non-functional write path look healthy.
  app/api/telemetry/route.ts        HTTP adapter, and nothing else.
  app/api/telemetry/resume/route.ts what the platform already holds, so an edge
                            coming back from an outage replays from max_seq
                            instead of resending four hours of 1 Hz samples.
  scripts/issue-device-key.mjs      mint a credential. Prints the key once.

The delivery contract, stated in one place (lib/telemetry/ingest.ts): the edge
assigns `seq` per (robot_id, job_id), buffers, and retries until each event
comes back accepted, duplicate or rejected. All three are terminal, so a
malformed event can never wedge the queue — which is what a 500 with no
per-event answer did before.

Three refusals worth naming. A credential may only speak for its own robot: one
leaked key must not forge the quality record of the whole fleet. A job in
another tenant answers exactly as a job that does not exist, so a valid key
cannot enumerate the platform. And provenance comes from the credential's
registered kind, never from the request body — a simulator cannot claim
measurement even if it asks to.

Status codes are chosen so a machine can branch without parsing prose: 200
processed, 401 bad key, 413 split the batch, 422 the whole batch is poison, 503
not configured. 422 rather than 200 for a wholly-rejected batch is deliberate —
a 200 there makes a machine emitting nothing but garbage indistinguishable,
to every dashboard, from one running perfectly.

No AI anywhere in this path. It decides whether a row is admissible and stores
it exactly as sent. Anything that interprets a reading belongs downstream of
the stored truth, never in front of it.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_014pRDhZ6XzDZyaeJoUR5Xuo
